# SPECIMEN: button chrome - the look-question that gates gui_flat_button.
#
# CINEMATIC_PLAN.md wants a "flat" control: text on a background, no engine skin, usable as a
# hot zone over a face or a rundown tile. Before building one it is worth knowing what the
# existing controls actually look like, because `Button.background_color` ALREADY exists and
# is drawn as send_gui_colorbutton + send_gui_text. If a colorbutton is a flat fill, the flat
# button essentially exists and the work is ergonomics and states; if it carries the same
# image chrome, it does not, and the clickregion is the only skin-free widget.
#
# Only an eye can answer that, which is what this range is for.
#
# The second question is the palette one: a clickregion (and, it seems, a colorbutton) carries
# a PSEUDO-HOVER highlight. A tint over a bright fill may be invisible, so the backgrounds
# below run light to dark deliberately - hover each one and see where the highlight still
# reads. That decides the default background for flat furniture.
#
# Drawn over the live 3D view on purpose: overlay furniture will sit over exactly that, and
# draw order against a moving background is not a thing a unit test can judge.

@map/visual_button_chrome "Visual: button chrome"
" Plain button vs background_color at five tones - is a colorbutton flat, and where does hover read?
    visual_set_index_by_name("button_chrome")
    await task_schedule(run_visual_button_chrome)
    ->END


=== run_visual_button_chrome
metadata: ``` yaml
type: visual/button_chrome
```
    # Something moving behind the controls, so draw order and legibility are both judgeable.
    _subject = to_object(npc_spawn(0, 0, 0, "Backdrop", "tsn", "starbase_command", "behav_station"))
    for _bi in range(9):
        terrain_spawn(0 - 6000 + _bi * 1500, 0, 3000, f"ref{_bi}", "#,ref", "plain_asteroid_9", "behav_asteroid")
    _anchor = visual_anchor(0, 0, 0)
    visual_camera(_anchor, lens=(0, 1500, 0 - 4200))

    # First row is the control: a plain gui_button, whatever the engine gives it. The rest set
    # background_color, which routes through colorbutton + text.
    _btns = ~~[
        ["plain gui_button (no background)", ""],
        ["background #eee (light)", "#eee"],
        ["background #888 (mid)", "#888"],
        ["background #222 (dark)", "#222"],
        ["background #1576 (translucent)", "#1576"],
        ["background #0000 (transparent)", "#0000"],
    ]~~
    visual_widgets(_btns)

    _expect = ~~[
        "row 1 is a normal button - note its skin: border, gradient, whatever the engine draws",
        "rows 2-6 set background_color: do they still carry that skin, or are they FLAT fills?",
        "if rows 2-6 are flat, the flat button already exists and only needs a name",
        "hover each row: where does the highlight still read - over light, mid, dark, all?",
        "row 6 is transparent: is it invisible, or does hover alone reveal it?",
    ]~~
    visual_case("Button chrome", _expect, subtitle="gates gui_flat_button (CINEMATIC_PLAN Phase 6)", notes="Button.background_color is drawn as colorbutton + text - this asks whether a colorbutton is skinned", data="6 rows, over a live 3D view so draw order is judgeable too")

    visual_expect("chrome: backdrop spawned", _subject is not None)
    visual_expect("chrome: six controls declared", len(visual_widget_specs()) == 6)
    ->END
